["<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>You cannot hunt deer until you can see deer country. Learn the look of the land, the plants and the sign, and every walk turns into scouting.</p></aside><ul><li>This is a photo walk. Look first, then read the bullets under each set of photos.</li><li>Three kinds of deer country near our bases: Kamloops grassland and fir edge, burns and cut blocks up the North Thompson, and the wet coast forest around Mission (Tip).</li><li>Mule deer and white tails live in the Interior; the Columbian black tailed deer lives on the coast and in the Fraser Valley <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Plants here are the ones from <a href=\"#/s/food-water-cover\">Food, water and cover</a>. Now you see them.</li><li>Photos come from many places with the same plants. Captions say where (Tip).</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-lac-du-bois-grassland.jpg\" alt=\"Lac du Bois grassland north of Kamloops in October. Open bunchgrass, few trees.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Lac du Bois grassland north of Kamloops in October. Open bunchgrass, few trees. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: tuchodi, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lac_du_Bois_Grasslands_Protected_Area_(4043429977).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-grass-fir-edge.jpg\" alt=\"The edge: grass meets Douglas fir. Deer feed in the open and bed in the trees.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The edge: grass meets Douglas fir. Deer feed in the open and bed in the trees. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Andres Alvarado, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_colorful_of_Lac_Du_Bois_Grasslands.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-lac-du-bois-potholes.jpg\" alt=\"Rolling grassland with small pothole lakes and fir patches on the ridges.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Rolling grassland with small pothole lakes and fir patches on the ridges. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Straitgate, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LacDuBoisBC.JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-kamloops-sagebrush.jpg\" alt=\"Sagebrush slope on the Mara trail, Kamloops. Grey shrubs, bare soil between.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Sagebrush slope on the Mara trail, Kamloops. Grey shrubs, bare soil between. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Ghune, CC0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grassland.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Look for the line where grass meets timber. That edge is where mule deer feed at dusk and dawn (Tip).</li></ul>","<ul><li>Winter range is the steep south and west facing slopes with old Douglas fir, often below about 760 m north of Kamloops <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Draws and gullies hold shrubs, shade and the last green feed in a dry fall <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Lac du Bois itself is a no shooting area inside the City of Kamloops boundary <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span>. It is a classroom, not a hunting spot (Tip).</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-bitterbrush-slope.jpg\" alt=\"Bitterbrush in bloom along a dry pine slope, Nevada. Same shrub as the Okanagan.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Bitterbrush in bloom along a dry pine slope, Nevada. Same shrub as the Okanagan. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Pacific Southwest Region, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blooming_shrubs_on_hiking_trail_in_Nevada_(49867903218).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-bitterbrush-leaf.jpg\" alt=\"Bitterbrush up close. Tiny wedge shaped leaves with 3 teeth at the tip.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Bitterbrush up close. Tiny wedge shaped leaves with 3 teeth at the tip. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Matt Lavin from Bozeman, Montana, USA, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Purshia_tridentata_(4036546795).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-sagebrush-bush.jpg\" alt=\"Big sagebrush. Silver grey, smells strong when you crush a leaf.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Big sagebrush. Silver grey, smells strong when you crush a leaf. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Z3lvs, CC0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Big_Sagebrush_(Artemisia_tridentata)_Washoe_Lake_State_Park,_Nevada_1.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-ceanothus-patch.jpg\" alt=\"Snowbrush on an open slope. Shiny leaves, white flower clusters in early summer.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Snowbrush on an open slope. Shiny leaves, white flower clusters in early summer. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Matt Lavin, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ceanothus_velutinus_002_%E2%80%94_Matt_Lavin.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-ceanothus-leaf.jpg\" alt=\"Snowbrush leaf: glossy, sticky, 3 strong veins from the base.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Snowbrush leaf: glossy, sticky, 3 strong veins from the base. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Thayne Tuason, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ceanothus_velutinus_var._velutinus_1.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Bitterbrush: grey green shrub on dry slopes, tiny 3 toothed leaves <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>. Key mule deer browse on dry grassland <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>","<ul><li>Sagebrush: deer eat little of it, but it marks the dry, low country deer use in winter <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Snowbrush (ceanothus) springs up after fire and logging and is good deer browse <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>. Source: E Flora BC and Parish and others, Plants of Southern Interior BC.</li></ul><ul><li>Look for twigs that end in a torn, ragged tip. Deer have no top front teeth, so they tear <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-saskatoon-flowers.jpg\" alt=\"Saskatoon in flower, Manning Park BC. White starry flowers, oval toothed leaves.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Saskatoon in flower, Manning Park BC. White starry flowers, oval toothed leaves. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Robert Flogaus-Faust, CC BY 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amelanchier_alnifolia_2_RF.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-saskatoon-berries.jpg\" alt=\"Saskatoon berries forming in early summer on a dry slope.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Saskatoon berries forming in early summer on a dry slope. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Thayne Tuason, CC BY 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amelanchier-alnifolia-var-cusickii-unripe-fruit_8795424698_o_2.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-woods-rose-hips.jpg\" alt=\"Wild rose hips in fall. Red, oval, on thorny stems.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Wild rose hips in fall. Red, oval, on thorny stems. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Jim Morefield from Nevada, USA, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Western_wildrose,_Rosa_woodsii_(16469499462).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-snowberry.jpg\" alt=\"Snowberry. White berries that hang on into winter.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Snowberry. White berries that hang on into winter. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: NPS Photo, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:File-Symphoricarpos_albus_-_native;-Blooms_June_-_July;_(9352ce2f-155d-4519-3e7c-5033029f6900).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-dogwood-stems.jpg\" alt=\"Red osier dogwood in a wet draw. Red stems stand out after the leaves drop.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Red osier dogwood in a wet draw. Red stems stand out after the leaves drop. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Matt Lavin, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cornus_sericea_-_red_osier_dogwood_-_53066633106.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Saskatoon: shrub 1 to 4 m, oval leaves toothed on the top half, purple berries in July <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. Top deer browse all year <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>","<ul><li>Rose: thorny stems and red hips; hips and twigs are eaten in fall and winter <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Red osier dogwood: bright red stems, white berries, wet ground; a favourite fall and winter browse <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>A draw with these shrubs plus water is a deer kitchen. Glass it at first light (Tip).</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-north-thompson-burn.jpg\" alt=\"Burned slope above the North Thompson River near Barriere. New green feed grows under the snags.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Burned slope above the North Thompson River near Barriere. New green feed grows under the snags. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Adam Jones, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scorched_section_of_forest_along_North_Thompson_River_near_Barriere,_BC.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-wells-gray-cutblock.jpg\" alt=\"Old cut block near Trophy Meadows, Wells Gray area. Young trees, shrubs and grass on the edges.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Old cut block near Trophy Meadows, Wells Gray area. Young trees, shrubs and grass on the edges. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Jason Hollinger, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Big_Bertha_(3818285480).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-douglas-fir-stand.jpg\" alt=\"Interior Douglas fir stand. Thick old trees make shade in fall and shelter in snow.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Interior Douglas fir stand. Thick old trees make shade in fall and shelter in snow. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Matt Lavin from Bozeman, Montana, USA, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pseudotsuga_menziesii_subsp._glauca_(Interior_Douglas-fir),_Wild_Horse_Island,_Montana_1.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-douglas-fir-tree.jpg\" alt=\"A lone Douglas fir on a dry grass slope. Soft needles, thick furrowed bark.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A lone Douglas fir on a dry grass slope. Soft needles, thick furrowed bark. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Matt Lavin, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pseudotsuga_menziesii_-_Douglas_fir_-_52487422990.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-kinnikinnick.jpg\" alt=\"Kinnikinnick, a low mat with red berries on dry, rocky ground under pine and fir.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Kinnikinnick, a low mat with red berries on dry, rocky ground under pine and fir. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Matt Lavin from Bozeman, Montana, USA, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arctostaphylos_uva-ursi_(5192873642).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>After a fire or logging, shrubs and forbs sprout for years. Burns and cut blocks are feed <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>","<ul><li>Mule deer often stay high in September in subalpine meadows, old burns and cut blocks <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Douglas fir: soft flat needles, cones with 3 pointed bracts <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. Winter food and thermal cover <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Kinnikinnick: evergreen mat, leathery spoon shaped leaves, red berries <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. It tells you the ground is dry and open (Tip).</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-salal.jpg\" alt=\"Salal, the shrub that covers the coast forest floor. Thick oval leaves.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Salal, the shrub that covers the coast forest floor. Thick oval leaves. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Robert Flogaus-Faust, CC BY 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gaultheria_shallon_RF.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-salmonberry.jpg\" alt=\"Salmonberry. Orange or red berries in early summer, wet ground.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Salmonberry. Orange or red berries in early summer, wet ground. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Walter Siegmund (talk), CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rubus_spectabilis_1564.JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-bracken.jpg\" alt=\"Bracken fern on a cut block edge.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Bracken fern on a cut block edge. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: brewbooks from near Seattle, USA, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pteridium_aquilinum_(bracken_fern)_-_Flickr_-_brewbooks.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-blackberry.jpg\" alt=\"Himalayan blackberry thicket. Cover for deer and bears on Fraser Valley edges.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Himalayan blackberry thicket. Cover for deer and bears on Fraser Valley edges. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Thayne Tuason, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rubus_armeniacus_1.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Black tailed deer fall and winter food: salal, red huckleberry, trailing blackberry, Douglas fir, western red cedar, deer fern and tree lichens <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>","<ul><li>Spring and summer: grasses, fireweed, salmonberry, willow and maple <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Thick second growth and blackberry tangles are cover; cut block and power line edges are feed (Tip).</li></ul><ul><li>Winter pushes coast deer to low, south facing slopes and floodplains with little snow <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-deer-track-dirt.jpg\" alt=\"Deer track near Wenatchee, Washington, in dry ground like the Okanagan. Two pointed toes.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Deer track near Wenatchee, Washington, in dry ground like the Okanagan. Two pointed toes. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Thayne Tuason, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deer_tracks_near_Saddlerock_Wenatchee_Washington.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-deer-track-foothills.jpg\" alt=\"Deer track in soft soil. The split heart shape points the way the deer walked.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Deer track in soft soil. The split heart shape points the way the deer walked. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Thayne Tuason, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deer_Tracks_at_Jacobson_Preserve_Wenatchee_Foothills.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-deer-trail-reeds.jpg\" alt=\"A deer trail pushed through grass. Packed soil and tracks in the middle.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A deer trail pushed through grass. Packed soil and tracks in the middle. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: MaryC2000, CC BY 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deer_Trail.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-deer-trail-snow.jpg\" alt=\"Deer tracks on a snowy trail. One line, prints close together: walking.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Deer tracks on a snowy trail. One line, prints close together: walking. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Peter Cooper Jr., CC0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deer_tracks_in_snow_on_trail_at_McKinstry_Brook_WMA_2026-02-01.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>A deer track is a split heart, pointed end forward, about 5 to 9 cm long for an adult <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>","<ul><li>Trails run from beds to feed to water. Follow one and you learn the daily loop (Tip).</li></ul><ul><li>Fresh: sharp edges, no debris in the print. Old: crumbled edges, leaves or rain marks inside <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>In snow, deep drag marks between prints mean deep snow and a tired deer. Leave winter range deer alone (Tip).</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-deer-pellets-fresh.jpg\" alt=\"Fresh deer pellets: dark, shiny and moist.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Fresh deer pellets: dark, shiny and moist. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Steve Redman ( MORA ), Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deer_scat_taken_in_2009_(b7b62065560e4e9095f9f86778c8b7b1).JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-deer-pellets-old.jpg\" alt=\"Old deer pellets: dry, pale and crumbly.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Old deer pellets: dry, pale and crumbly. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Rosendahl, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deer_poop.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-deer-rub.jpg\" alt=\"A buck rub: bark stripped from a small tree at knee to waist height.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A buck rub: bark stripped from a small tree at knee to waist height. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Wasp32, CC BY 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deer_rub.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Deer pellets are about 1 to 1.5 cm long, oval, often with a point at one end <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul>","<ul><li>Shiny and soft means hours old; dull, hard and pale means days to weeks <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>. Many piles in late fall mark winter range <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Clumped pellets mean soft green feed; separate pellets mean dry browse <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Rubs: bucks strip bark off saplings with their antlers from late summer through the rut <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. A line of rubs marks a buck's travel route (Tip).</li></ul><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Grandpa's rule (wisdom): Find the edge, find the water, find the shade. The deer are where all three touch.</p></aside>","<aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Walking the open grass at noon. The deer are bedded in the trees beside it.</li><li>Calling every twig a browse sign. Check for the ragged deer tear, not the clean hare cut.</li><li>Learning plants from photos only. Touch, crush and smell them once in the field.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>Walk one draw from bottom to top. Find saskatoon, rose and snowberry, one deer track and one pellet pile. Photograph each with your hand for scale. Then find the nearest water and the nearest fir patch.</p></aside>","<ul><li>BC Ministry of Environment, <a href=\"https://www.env.gov.bc.ca/wld/documents/muledeer.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mule and Black tailed Deer in BC</a> (2000), checked 2026-10-03 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>: winter range, coast diet, snow.</li><li>Willms 1976, <a href=\"https://cdnsciencepub.com/doi/pdf/10.4141/cjas76-065\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">deer diet near Kamloops</a>, checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>: winter range and Douglas fir.</li><li>Parish, Coupé and Lloyd, Plants of Southern Interior British Columbia (Lone Pine), and <a href=\"https://linnet.geog.ubc.ca/biodiversity/eflora/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">E Flora BC</a> plant pages: plant identification features (75% to 85%).</li><li>Elbroch, Mammal Tracks and Sign (Stackpole, 2003): track size, pellet size, browse sign (75% to 80%).</li><li>BC Hunting and Trapping Regulations Synopsis 2026 to 2028, Region 3 page 33: no discharge in Lac du Bois and the City of Kamloops <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span>.</li><li>Photos: Wikimedia Commons, credits under each photo and on the Photo credits page. Checked 2026-10-04.</li></ul>"]